steps.body:
Every request that reaches Roombook either finds the one handler meant for it, or gets a clear answer that it took a wrong turn. That's routing, and getting it right is what makes an API predictable: the same path always means the same thing, and a mistake gets the status code that says exactly what was wrong.

## Method plus pattern

A **route** pairs a method with a **path pattern** and points to a handler. A pattern can have a **path parameter**, a named piece that stands for any value: `/v1/rooms/{roomId}` matches `/v1/rooms/3` and `/v1/rooms/42`, and hands your handler the 3 or the 42 as `roomId`. The app's whole list of routes is its **route table**.

```diagram routes-and-methods-route-table
The request matches one row by its method and its path, and that row's handler runs.
```

Spring writes patterns exactly as the diagram does, with the parameter in braces. When the app starts, Spring MVC reads every controller's annotations and builds this table. Each request then arrives at the **DispatcherServlet**, Spring MVC's front door, which looks the request up in the table and calls the matching method.

## A controller per feature

Roombook's room routes live in `rooms/RoomController.java`:

```java sketch
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/v1/rooms")
public class RoomController {

    private final InMemoryRoomRepository rooms;

    public RoomController(InMemoryRoomRepository rooms) {
        this.rooms = rooms;
    }

    @GetMapping
    public Map<String, List<Room>> list() { ... }

    @GetMapping("/{roomId}")
    public ResponseEntity<Object> get(@PathVariable String roomId) { ... }

    @PostMapping
    public ResponseEntity<Room> create(@RequestBody RoomInput input) { ... }

    @PutMapping("/{roomId}")
    public ResponseEntity<Object> replace(@PathVariable String roomId, @RequestBody RoomInput input) { ... }

    @PatchMapping("/{roomId}")
    public ResponseEntity<Object> update(@PathVariable String roomId, @RequestBody RoomInput patch) { ... }

    @DeleteMapping("/{roomId}")
    public ResponseEntity<Object> delete(@PathVariable String roomId) { ... }
}
```

`@RequestMapping("/v1/rooms")` on the class puts every route in it under that path, so `@GetMapping("/{roomId}")` means `GET /v1/rooms/{roomId}`. There's one annotation per method: `@GetMapping`, `@PostMapping`, `@PutMapping`, `@PatchMapping`, and `@DeleteMapping`. The constructor asks for the repository that holds the rooms, and Spring passes in its one bean of that class, which the lesson on layers explains.

Every Roombook route sits under `/v1`, for version 1, as the lesson on what an API is described. When a change would break existing clients one day, a `/v2` can live next to it while they move over, which the lesson on OpenAPI covers. `/healthz` stays outside, since it isn't part of the API's contract.

## Reading a path parameter

`@PathVariable String roomId` gives the handler the parameter with the same name. The obvious way is to ask for a number, `@PathVariable long roomId`, and Spring converts the text for you. The catch is what it does when it can't: for `/v1/rooms/abc`, it answers 400 Bad Request, as the second example shows, which says the request was malformed. Roombook's contract says otherwise. From the client's side, `/v1/rooms/abc` is simply an address where nothing lives, so it's a 404, the same as an id that's valid but missing.

So Roombook takes the text, and `common/Ids.java` turns it into a number, or into nothing:

```java
public static Optional<Long> parse(String text) {
    if (!text.matches("[1-9][0-9]{0,17}")) {
        return Optional.empty();
    }
    return Optional.of(Long.parseLong(text));
}
```

The pattern accepts digits only, with no leading zero, and at most 18 of them, so the number always fits in a `long`. `abc`, `-1`, `1.5`, and `007` all fail.

## Answering 404 yourself

When the room isn't there, the handler answers with a small JSON body of its own, built by `common/ApiError.java`:

```java
public record ApiError(int status, String title) {

    public static ResponseEntity<Object> response(HttpStatus status, String title) {
        return ResponseEntity.status(status).body(new ApiError(status.value(), title));
    }
}
```

`ResponseEntity` is Spring's class for a whole response: status, headers, and body. Every room route looks the room up the same way, and answers `{"status": 404, "title": "Room not found"}` when it's missing:

```java
private Optional<Room> find(String roomId) {
    return Ids.parse(roomId).flatMap(rooms::findById);
}

private static ResponseEntity<Object> notFound() {
    return ApiError.response(HttpStatus.NOT_FOUND, "Room not found");
}
```

This is a first draft of Roombook's errors. Each handler builds its own, and the lesson on validation and errors replaces them with one format, Problem Details, that every error shares.

## 404 or 405, for free

A request can miss in two ways. If no pattern matches the path, it's 404. If the path matches but not the method, like `POST /v1/rooms/1`, the thing exists and the method is wrong, and the answer is **405 Method Not Allowed**, with an `Allow` header listing the methods that do work. Spring sends both by itself, as the third example shows, in Spring Boot's own error format: a `timestamp`, the `status`, the `error`, a `message`, and the `path`.

While you develop, DevTools also adds a `trace` to those answers, the long list of Java methods that led to the error, thousands of characters each time. Roombook turns it off in `application.yaml`, so error answers look as they will in production. Spring Boot 4 renamed the setting to `spring.web.error.include-stacktrace`, so older guides call it `server.error.include-stacktrace`:

```yaml
spring:
  web:
    error:
      include-stacktrace: never
```

The middleware lesson explains why a stack trace should never reach a client.

## The most specific pattern wins

If Roombook added `/v1/rooms/popular` next to `/v1/rooms/{roomId}`, both would match `/v1/rooms/popular`. Spring picks the more specific one, the pattern with fewer parameters, no matter which you wrote first, so fixed paths and parameters can sit side by side safely. Patterns can do more, too: `{roomId:[0-9]+}` matches only digits, and `{*path}` captures the rest of the path.

Most of an API's traffic is reads: people look far more often than they change anything. And every program between your API and its clients, from browsers to caches, treats a GET as harmless. They repeat it, prefetch it, and keep copies of it. So the rules for reads are the ones you can least afford to break.

## Read one, read many

Roombook has two reads for rooms. `GET /v1/rooms/{roomId}` answers **200 OK** with one room, or 404. `GET /v1/rooms` answers 200 with every room:

```java
@GetMapping
public Map<String, List<Room>> list() {
    return Map.of("items", rooms.list());
}
```

The list is wrapped in an object, `{"items": [...]}`, instead of being sent as a bare array. That leaves room to add fields next to it later without breaking anyone: the next lesson adds `nextCursor`, for reading a long list a page at a time. A bare array can't grow that way. An empty list is still a success: 200 with `{"items": []}`, never 404, because the list exists, it just has nothing in it yet.

Here's the read for one room:

```java
@GetMapping("/{roomId}")
public ResponseEntity<Object> get(@PathVariable String roomId) {
    Optional<Room> room = find(roomId);
    if (room.isEmpty()) {
        return notFound();
    }
    return ResponseEntity.ok(room.get());
}
```

`find` gives an `Optional`, a value that may be empty, recall from your Java course: `Ids.parse` makes one from the text, and `flatMap` looks the id up only if there is one. An empty result means no such room, and the handler returns the 404. Otherwise `ResponseEntity.ok` sends the room with a 200. The return type is `ResponseEntity<Object>`, since the body is either a `Room` or an `ApiError`.

## Where the rooms live, for now

`rooms.findById` and `rooms.list` come from `rooms/InMemoryRoomRepository.java`. A **repository** is the one place in the code that stores and fetches a kind of thing, here rooms. For now it keeps them in memory, filled with Roombook's three rooms when the server starts:

```java
@Repository
public class InMemoryRoomRepository {

    private final Map<Long, Room> rooms = new ConcurrentSkipListMap<>(Map.of(
            1L, new Room(1, "Turing", "main-library", 6, List.of("whiteboard", "screen")),
            2L, new Room(2, "Lovelace", "main-library", 4, List.of("whiteboard")),
            3L, new Room(3, "Hopper", "science-library", 8, List.of("screen", "video-call"))));
    private final AtomicLong nextId = new AtomicLong(4);
```

`@Repository` makes it a bean, so there's exactly one, shared by every request. Recall from the first project lesson that every request runs on its own thread, so this map is used by many threads at once. `ConcurrentSkipListMap` is safe for that, and keeps the rooms sorted by id, and `AtomicLong` hands out new ids without two threads ever getting the same one. A plain `HashMap` here would break, rarely and unpredictably, under load.

Memory forgets everything when the server restarts, which DevTools does on every compile, so your changes vanish each time. That's fine while you learn routing. The lesson on layers explains why repositories are a separate layer, and the lesson on raw SQL swaps this one for PostgreSQL without touching the controller.

## Safe means no changes

Recall from the web course that GET is **safe**: it only reads. For an API, this is a promise other programs rely on. A chat app fetches every link someone pastes to show a preview. A browser may load links before you click them, to be quick. A client retries a GET that timed out. If `GET /v1/bookings/1001/cancel` cancelled a booking, pasting that link in a group chat would cancel it. So a GET never changes anything a client can see. Writing a log line, or counting visits, is fine.

## HEAD

**HEAD** asks for exactly what GET would answer, without the body: the same status and the same headers. A client uses it to check that something exists, or how big it is, without downloading it. Spring answers HEAD with your GET handler and leaves the body out, so you get it for free.

## 200 or 404

A read answers 200 with the thing, or 404 when it isn't there. Later lessons add two more: 304 Not Modified, when the client's copy is still current, in the next lesson, and 403 or 404 for something the caller isn't allowed to see, in the lesson on permissions.

Creating is where a client most needs a clear answer: did it work, what's the new thing's id, and where can it be read later. POST is also the one common method that's never safe to repeat, which makes it the method behind most double bookings and double charges on the web.

## Create: 201 with Location

`POST /v1/rooms` creates a room from the JSON in the request's body:

```java
@PostMapping
public ResponseEntity<Room> create(@RequestBody RoomInput input) {
    Room room = rooms.insert(input);
    return ResponseEntity.created(URI.create("/v1/rooms/" + room.id())).body(room);
}
```

`@RequestBody RoomInput input` tells Spring to read the body as JSON and turn it into a `RoomInput`, a record with only the fields a client may send:

```java
public record RoomInput(String name, String building, Integer capacity, List<String> amenities) {
}
```

The next lesson covers bodies in full. A separate record for the input matters: there's no `id` in it, so a client that sends `"id": 1` can't overwrite room 1, since Jackson has nowhere to put it and ignores it. The values aren't checked yet, so a missing capacity arrives as `null`, which is why it's an `Integer` and not an `int`. The lesson on validation and errors checks every field.

`ResponseEntity.created(uri)` sets the status and the `Location` header in one call, and the answer has three parts that clients use:

- **201 Created**: something new exists.
- A `Location` header with the new room's address, `/v1/rooms/4`, where the client can read it from now on.
- The new room in the body, so the client learns its id, and any value the server filled in, without a second request.

You'll also see `ServletUriComponentsBuilder` used here, which builds a full URL from the request, like `http://localhost:8080/v1/rooms/4`. A path alone is valid in `Location`, and it stays right behind proxies that change the host.

## POST isn't idempotent

Recall from the web course that sending the same POST twice does the work twice. Two identical `POST /v1/rooms` requests make two rooms. For bookings, it's worse. A student taps Book on a train, the request reaches Roombook, the booking is made, and the answer is lost in a tunnel. The app sees a timeout and sends the request again, and now the student holds two bookings. The reliability lesson fixes this with an `Idempotency-Key` header, so Roombook can tell a retry from a new request.

## Actions: POST /v1/bookings/{bookingId}/cancel

Some changes aren't creating a thing or editing a field. Cancelling a booking has rules: only before it starts, only by its owner or staff in that building. It also has effects: the room frees up, and an email goes out. Roombook models it as an **action**: a POST to a path named with a verb, under the booking it acts on. In `bookings/BookingController.java`:

```java
@PostMapping("/{bookingId}/cancel")
public ResponseEntity<Object> cancel(@PathVariable String bookingId) {
    Optional<Booking> found = find(bookingId);
    if (found.isEmpty()) {
        return notFound();
    }
    Booking booking = found.get();
    if (booking.status() == BookingStatus.CANCELLED) {
        return ResponseEntity.ok(booking);
    }
    return ResponseEntity.ok(bookings.cancel(booking.id()));
}
```

A missing booking gets `{"status": 404, "title": "Booking not found"}`, the same first draft as for rooms.

The repository's `cancel` sets the status to `cancelled` and adds 1 to the booking's `version`, as every change does, which the lesson on data integrity uses to catch two edits that clash.

Why not the other methods? Not DELETE, because the booking isn't gone: it stays, with its status set to `cancelled`, so the history shows it. Not `PATCH {"status": "cancelled"}`, because then a client could just as easily send `{"status": "confirmed"}` to bring back a booking whose slot someone else now holds. An action runs the process with its rules, and a PATCH edits fields. The answer is 200 with the cancelled booking.

This action is safe to retry, even though POST in general isn't: a booking that's already cancelled comes back as it is, unchanged. Design actions this way whenever you can.

## 202: finished later

Some work takes too long to finish while the client waits, like exporting a whole term of bookings to a spreadsheet. The API answers **202 Accepted** at once, with a `Location` pointing at a resource that reports progress, and `ResponseEntity.accepted()` builds it. The client checks that address until the work is done, and it then links to the result. Roombook's booking emails work the same way inside the server, with no 202 to the client: the booking gets its 201 straight away, and the email goes out moments later, through the background jobs of the Redis lesson.

There are two ways to change something that exists, and they behave very differently. Use the wrong one, or build one wrong, and a client that meant to change one field wipes out three others. Knowing exactly what each one promises is what lets clients update safely.

## PUT replaces the whole thing

A **PUT** sends a complete new version of the resource, and the server stores exactly that, in place of the old one:

```java
@PutMapping("/{roomId}")
public ResponseEntity<Object> replace(@PathVariable String roomId, @RequestBody RoomInput input) {
    Optional<Room> room = find(roomId);
    if (room.isEmpty()) {
        return notFound();
    }
    long id = room.get().id();
    return ResponseEntity.ok(rooms.update(
            new Room(id, input.name(), input.building(), input.capacity(), input.amenities())));
}
```

Every field comes from the body, and only the id comes from the room that's there, so the path decides which room, never the body. A field the client leaves out is gone, as the third example shows: a PUT with only a name leaves a room with no capacity at all. A real API refuses a PUT that's missing required fields, with 422, and the lesson on validation and errors adds that check.

PUT is **idempotent**: sending the same PUT twice leaves the room exactly as one did. That makes it safe for a client to retry.

## PATCH changes some fields

A **PATCH** sends only the changes. Roombook follows **JSON Merge Patch**, a standard set of rules for what a JSON patch means, written down as RFC 7396:

- A field in the patch replaces the current value.
- A field left out stays as it is.
- A field set to `null` is removed, or cleared.
- Arrays are replaced whole. To add an amenity, you send the full new list.
- Objects inside the resource are merged field by field, by the same rules.

A merge patch announces itself with `Content-Type: application/merge-patch+json`. Spring reads it with no extra setup, since Jackson accepts `application/json` and every type ending in `+json`.

```java
@PatchMapping("/{roomId}")
public ResponseEntity<Object> update(@PathVariable String roomId, @RequestBody RoomInput patch) {
    Optional<Room> found = find(roomId);
    if (found.isEmpty()) {
        return notFound();
    }
    Room room = found.get();
    return ResponseEntity.ok(rooms.update(new Room(
            room.id(),
            requireNonNullElse(patch.name(), room.name()),
            requireNonNullElse(patch.building(), room.building()),
            requireNonNullElse(patch.capacity(), room.capacity()),
            requireNonNullElse(patch.amenities(), room.amenities()))));
}
```

`requireNonNullElse(a, b)`, from `java.util.Objects`, gives `a`, or `b` when `a` is `null`, so each field keeps its current value when the patch doesn't have one.

## Missing or null

JSON can say two different things about a field: it's not there, or it's there and `null`. In a merge patch they mean different things: `{}` changes nothing, and `{"note": null}` clears a booking's note. A record can't tell them apart: Jackson sets a missing field to `null` too, so both arrive the same. That's fine for a room, since none of its fields may ever be null, and the lesson on validation and errors turns `{"capacity": null}` into a clear error. For a field that can be cleared, like a booking's note, the handler has to look at the JSON itself, and the next lesson shows how.

## Is PATCH idempotent?

A merge patch is: sending `{"capacity": 8}` twice still leaves 8. But HTTP doesn't promise it for PATCH in general, since a patch format could say "add 2 to the capacity", which changes the result every time. So the method table in the next part says PATCH isn't idempotent, and clients don't retry one blindly.

## 200 or 204

After an update, an API answers either **200** with the updated resource, or **204 No Content** with no body. Roombook answers 200, so the client sees exactly what's stored, including anything the server changed, like a booking's version number. Pick one and use it for every update.
